To fulfill the requirements for the xv6 File System Recovery project, I have provided the necessary modifications to the xv6 kernel, the user-level recovery programs, and the external Python recovery tool.

### 1. Changes to xv6 Functions and Header Files

To allow user-level programs to inspect the file system at a low level, we must add a system call that bypasses the standard file abstraction.

#### A. `kernel/fs.h`
We define a structure to pass raw inode data to user space.
```c
// Add to kernel/fs.h
struct inode_info {
  uint type;
  short major;
  short minor;
  short nlink;
  uint size;
  uint addrs[13];
};
```

#### B. `kernel/sysfile.c`
Implement the system call logic to read a disk inode (`dinode`) by index.
```c
// Add to kernel/sysfile.c
uint64
sys_get_dinode(void)
{
  int inum;
  uint64 addr; // user pointer to struct inode_info
  struct buf *bp;
  struct dinode *dip;

  if(argint(0, &inum) < 0 || argaddr(1, &addr) < 0)
    return -1;

  // Read the block containing the inode
  bp = bread(1, IBLOCK(inum, sb));
  dip = (struct dinode*)bp->data + (inum % IPB);

  struct inode_info info;
  info.type = dip->type;
  info.nlink = dip->nlink;
  info.size = dip->size;
  memmove(info.addrs, dip->addrs, sizeof(info.addrs));

  brelse(bp);

  if(copyout(myproc()->pagetable, addr, (char *)&info, sizeof(info)) < 0)
    return -1;
  return 0;
}
```

#### C. Plumbing (Syscall Registration)
*   **`kernel/syscall.h`**: `#define SYS_get_dinode 22`
*   **`kernel/syscall.c`**: Add `[SYS_get_dinode] sys_get_dinode,` to the syscall table.
*   **`user/user.h`**: `int get_dinode(int, struct inode_info*);`
*   **`user/usys.pl`**: `entry("get_dinode");`

